  "text": " It came down to Kenneth Walker and the Seahawks' ability to slow him down. I'm going to talk in a second about what they did specifically to slow down Kenneth Walker. But first, I want to throw in on this. Weeks 1 to 6, the Kansas City Chiefs, and specifically Kenneth Walker, 6.3 yards per carry, number one in the NFL. 665 rushing yards, most in the NFL. 14.2% of his runs were explosive gains, number one in the NFL. plus 0.16 EPA per rush, number one in the NFL, and a 45% success rate on early down runs. Now look to the next column over. You can see week seven versus the Seattle Seahawks, 6.3 yards per carry dropped all the way down to 2.6. He averaged only 0% explosive run rate, negative 0.04 EPA per rush, 36 total rushing yards, a 23% early down success rate. Everything was significantly worse in week seven than it was prior in the season. This was a massive step up And the thing is what was so elite about Mike McDonald and he done this before and this was a staple from last year as well But just to refresh your memory, he's the number one best run defense despite playing light boxes at one of the highest rates in the NFL. And that was on display in the game against Kenneth Walker as well. To stop Kenneth Walker, Seattle used a light box on 57% of his runs. Prior to that in the season, he faced a light box on just 20% of his runs. Seattle didn't go to the heavier boxes. They stayed very light, even going up against Kenneth Walker. In fact, they used 8-plus man box on only 21% of Kenneth Walker's runs. Prior to that on the season, 48% of his runs were against 8-plus man boxes. So this was just a massive change of pace. Most teams were loading the box, trying to slow down Kenneth Walker and really struggling to do that. Mike McDonald stuck to his normal defensive game plan, stayed lighter in the box than most every other team and massively shut down Kenneth Walker.",
